<div class="post-body">
<p>Readers leave pages that load slowly, and search engines notice. The good news: most slow pages are slow for the same few reasons.</p>
<h2 id="measure-first">Measure first <a class="heading-link" href="#measure-first" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Run the page through a speed test on a phone setting, not a desktop one. Write down the largest contentful paint, which is the time until the main content is visible. Under one second is great. Over two and a half seconds needs work.</p>
<h2 id="the-usual-causes-biggest-first">The usual causes, biggest first <a class="heading-link" href="#the-usual-causes-biggest-first" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<figure>
<a class="enlarge" href="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.webp"><picture>
<source type="image/webp" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/320.webp 320w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/480.webp 480w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/640.webp 640w, /blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.webp 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw">
<img src="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png" srcset="/blog/media/d9df18e6d7e8625d/800.png 800w" sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 48rem, 100vw" width="800" height="450" alt="Bar chart of a slow page&#39;s bytes: images take the most, then scripts, fonts, styles and HTML" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
</picture></a>
<figcaption>
<p>Where the bytes of a typical slow page go.</p>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<ol>
<li><strong>Images that are too large.</strong> A photo saved at 4,000 pixels wide for a slot 800 pixels wide wastes most of its bytes. Serve the size the page needs, in a modern format.</li>
<li><strong>Scripts the reader does not need.</strong> Every chat widget and tracking tag delays the page. Remove the ones nobody reads the data from.</li>
<li><strong>Fonts that block the text.</strong> Show the text in a system font first, and swap in the web font when it arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Layout that jumps.</strong> Give every image a width and height, so the text does not move while the page loads.</li>
</ol>
<p>Fix the first cause on your slowest page, measure again, and move on. Faster pages also get crawled more often, which helps <a href="/blog/what-a-sitemap-does/">your sitemap</a> do its job.</p>
<section class="faq">
<details open>
<summary>How fast should a page load?</summary>
<p>Aim for the main content to show within 2.5 seconds on a phone.</p>
</details>
<details open>
<summary>Which page should I fix first?</summary>
<p>The slowest page that brings in the most readers.</p>
</details>
</section>

</div>
